1
00:00:15,220 --> 00:00:20,180
The safe handling of compressed gases starts before the gases even get to the facility.

2
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:24,420
When cylinders containing compressed gas are delivered, a responsible person should confirm

3
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:28,960
that the cylinder's contents are properly labeled with what is expected in the order.

4
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:32,660
For example, if a factory is expecting chlorine to be delivered, the cylinder shouldn't

5
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:34,700
be labeled as hydrogen.

6
00:00:34,700 --> 00:00:38,980
Cylinders that arrive with no labeling at all should never be accepted into a building.

7
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:43,040
This person should also visually inspect the cylinder for any damage.

8
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,560
This should be in place and in working order with a valve protection cap.

9
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:52,200
Finally, the safety data sheet for each cylinder must be obtained, checked, and kept on file.

10
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:56,000
Once the cylinders have been received, they will need to be properly stored.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,040
Compressed gas cylinders should be stored in cool, dry, level, well-ventilated areas

12
00:01:00,040 --> 00:01:01,760
and fire-resistant areas.

13
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:05,960
All storage areas should meet the specifications of what is being stored, as well as following

14
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:09,140
local, state, and federal regulations.

15
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:13,580
Storage must also comply with compressed gas association standards.

16
00:01:13,580 --> 00:01:17,060
Compressed gas cylinders need to be kept away from heat sources and shouldn't be stored

17
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:22,060
in direct sunlight or anywhere that the temperature could reach about 125 degrees Fahrenheit.

18
00:01:22,060 --> 00:01:25,260
Cylinders should include a pressure relief system to prevent explosion in the case of

19
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:29,180
built-up or excess pressure due to fire or high temperatures.

20
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:32,820
All compressed gas cylinders must be stored upright and secured appropriately.

21
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:37,660
The use of bungee cords, zip ties, ropes, and wires isn't an acceptable means of

22
00:01:37,660 --> 00:01:39,620
securing cylinders.

23
00:01:39,620 --> 00:01:43,180
Cylinders shouldn't be stored near elevators or gangways or other places where objects can

24
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:44,420
fall on them.

25
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:48,460
They also shouldn't be stored near hallways, exits, and entrances, or heavy traffic areas

26
00:01:48,460 --> 00:01:51,340
where unauthorized personnel could tamper with them.

27
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:55,460
Once in the appropriate storage area, the names and hazards of each gas must be posted

28
00:01:55,460 --> 00:01:59,180
and cylinders must be separated and stored by hazard glass.

29
00:01:59,180 --> 00:02:03,260
For example, flammable gases shouldn't be stored near oxidizers.

30
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:07,060
Once the cylinder is empty or not in use, the valves must all be closed with a valve

31
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:10,540
protector cap in place and the regulator removed.

32
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:14,940
Empty cylinders should be labeled empty and stored separately from the full ones.

33
00:02:14,940 --> 00:02:18,820
Sometimes it's necessary to transport cylinders from one location to another.

34
00:02:18,820 --> 00:02:22,480
Doing so requires certain precautions to keep everyone safe.

35
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,740
All connected equipment, including regulators and piping, should be removed and all valves

36
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:28,580
secured before transport.

37
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:32,500
Never drag or roll compressed gas cylinders to move them.

38
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:35,420
Only an appropriate cart or hand truck should be used.

39
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:39,260
It's always important to assume that a cylinder is full when moving it, even if it's marked

40
00:02:39,260 --> 00:02:40,340
as empty.

41
00:02:40,340 --> 00:02:43,380
This is a better safe than sorry situation.

42
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:46,020
Only one cylinder should be moved at a time.

43
00:02:46,020 --> 00:02:50,660
If it's necessary to move more than one, a two cylinder car can be used and both cylinders

44
00:02:50,660 --> 00:02:53,180
need to be properly secured first.

45
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:56,500
Now that we've gotten the compressed gas cylinders stored where we need them, how

46
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:58,200
do we safely use them?

47
00:02:58,200 --> 00:03:01,940
The first step is making sure they're properly installed by the supplier or someone on site

48
00:03:01,940 --> 00:03:03,640
who's trained to do so.

49
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:08,040
Many personnel using compressed gas cylinders must be trained on all proper use and handling

50
00:03:08,040 --> 00:03:09,400
techniques.

51
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:12,580
These include labeling and using pressure relief valves.

52
00:03:12,580 --> 00:03:16,240
Before using the compressed gas cylinder, read all label information and the safety

53
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:18,280
data sheet for each cylinder.

54
00:03:18,280 --> 00:03:21,520
All fittings should be tested for leaks using a soap solution.

55
00:03:21,520 --> 00:03:26,120
This might go without saying, but an open flame should never be used to test for leaks.

56
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:28,400
Cylinder valves should always be accessible.

57
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,300
It's finally time to use the compressed gas.

58
00:03:31,300 --> 00:03:34,560
Cylinders must always be used in well ventilated areas.

59
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:39,860
Tubings should be checked regularly for integrity and any damaged tubing must be removed immediately.

60
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:45,220
Cylinders shouldn't be empty below 25 PSI to prevent backflow of air or other contaminants.

61
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:48,900
The compressed gas association recommends wearing personal protective equipment like

62
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:53,460
gloves, goggles, and protective clothing to minimize the risk of injury when using

63
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:54,460
cylinders.

64
00:03:54,460 --> 00:03:58,840
Unfortunately, despite all the precautions, accidents do sometimes happen.

65
00:03:58,840 --> 00:04:02,240
So what do you do in case of an emergency associated with compressed gas?

66
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,920
First, make sure to review and understand all emergency procedures ahead of time so you'll

67
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:09,160
know just what to do if needed.

68
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:12,280
Fire extinguishers should always be available and accessible.

69
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:15,560
Make sure you know at least two paths of exit in case of a fire.

70
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:18,600
One path might be blocked, so be ready to use the other one.

71
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:22,520
Eyewash and emergency showers must be nearby and tested regularly.

72
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:27,740
If a leak is detected, tell a supervisor immediately, warn others, and evacuate.

73
00:04:27,740 --> 00:04:32,220
If skin comes in contact with refrigerated liquefied gases, thaw the area immediately

74
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:34,860
using lukewarm water and seek medical attention.

75
00:04:34,860 --> 00:04:39,020
Remember, if all else fails, stay calm and call 911.

76
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,160
Following safe handling and use precautions is the best way to safely use compressed

77
00:04:43,160 --> 00:04:46,460
gas cylinders and hopefully prevent emergencies from happening.
